# REGRESSION — a CoW element view keeps working no matter WHICH NAME the
# collection was spelled through when the view was taken.
#
#     Vector[String] alias = v            # second name for the same collection
#     String s = alias.get(0).unwrap()    # view spelled through `alias`
#     String s = v.get(0).unwrap()        # view spelled through `v`
#
# Both spellings name the same storage, so both must survive `v` growing
# past a reallocation. Before the collection identity was resolved to the
# STORAGE OWNER at the two provenance producers, the `alias` spelling was
# recorded verbatim: the `source_mut_unsafe` query (keyed on the local's
# NAME) asked about "alias" while the prescan had recorded the mutations
# under "v", so the lazy rescue for `s` was never emitted and `s` read the
# freed buffer -- rc 139 on both backends, ASan heap-use-after-free in
# `gorget_string_copy_cow`.
#
# CELL: receiver spelling = 1-hop alias · view via alias, mutation via root ·
#       root shape = bare local · payload = heap-forced (`mk`) · element type
#       = String (the only type with a `borrow_view_fn` protocol).
#
# ⚠ THE REALLOC IS LOAD-BEARING: `v` must be pushed enough to force the
# backing store to reallocate (64x here). One push is rc 0 on both spellings
# and proves nothing. The payload's heap-ness is NOT load-bearing --
# `cow_alias_spelled_view_literal_payload.gg` is the same cell with a literal
# payload and reproduced the identical crash.
#
# MECHANISM PIN: the sibling harness assertion checks the emitted GIR carries
# `borrow_view` + `__cow_mat` for this program -- stdout alone cannot tell
# "the rescue ran" from "nothing needed rescuing".

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

void main():
    Vector[String] v = []
    v.push(mk("hel", "lo"))
    Vector[String] alias = v
    String s = alias.get(0).unwrap()
    int i = 0
    while i < 64:
        v.push(mk("fill", "er"))
        i = i + 1
    print(s)
